ur response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**The Calculated Risk of the Rocket**\n\nAlright, the user wants me to play Dou Dizhu as the Landlord. Here\'s what\'s going through my mind. I\'ve got a decent hand: a Rocket, trips (KKK), top pairs (AA), and a bunch of singles, but no 7, which hurts my straight potential. The right player just hit me with 222K. This is a tough opening move; it’s a power play. Do I use my Rocket now?\n\nPart of me screams "use it!" It’s the only way to beat that hand, and getting control back feels good. But then I look at my other cards. I\'ve got way too many singles, and using the Rocket means I have to lead with something that might just get me back to square one. Leading with KKK is my best option if I bomb, but it\'s not a killer move, it does not guarantee control. Then the next hand, they can just bomb and I am doomed.\n\nAnd here is the heart of the matter. If I pass, I take the risk of seeing the opponents play and save my best card. I don\'t love it, but passing means I see their hand. If the opponents do not beat 222 with a small card, and they start a sequence, I can drop a Rocket on them. 222 might be an attempt to take control. But if the opponents have the last two, and they play that out, I am done.\n\nOn the other hand, if I Rocket now, I am betting on a quick clearing of my hand. My best lead after the Rocket is AA. But 10-10 or 8-8 are easy counters, I have J and singles... If I get back control with a bad hand, I am done.\n\nThe thing is, I have 16 cards, they have 13. They are not close to done.\n\nThe prudent strategy here is to hold the Rocket. Let them lead. This move gives me a better look at their hand and allows me to react, or, at the very least, potentially save the Rocket for a situation where it guarantees victory or a stop. It\'s a risk, but a calculated one, and the numbers are in my favor. Time to Pass.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand contains a Rocket, a strong triplet (KKK), and a top pair (AA), but also several disconnected small singles (4, 5, 6, 9, J) with no 7 to connect a straight. The opponent (Right) has played 222K, a very powerful control hand, leaving only one 2 remaining in the deck.\nReason: To beat 222K, I must use my Rocket (BlackJoker + RedJoker). However, doing so at this early stage (Turn 2) with 16 cards in hand is risky. If I take the lead, I struggle to clear my small singles effectively; leading a single 4 or 5 puts me at a disadvantage, and leading KKK+x only clears one junk card while exposing me to bombs or leaving me with no re-entry. It is strategically better to Pass, saving the Rocket for a critical moment (like stopping a long sequence or a game-winning move) and hoping the opponent leads singles or pairs that allow me to play my intermediate cards or high pairs later.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
